-- emit: `==` on a `List`, which is the one derived `eq` S5 does NOT emit
-- (`docs/design/static-dispatch-spike.md` §9.5, §5.2, A.55, A.60, A.51).
--
-- `List a` is a `foreign type`: it has no constructors, so there is no body
-- to derive from, and the two loops of §9.5 belong in `core/List.js` behind
-- a `pub foreign eq` that §5.2 adds in S6. Until then the only function
-- that can answer is `core/Basics.js`'s `eq`, the one structural walk —
-- and it is taken only when every part of the comparison says the walk
-- would answer the same thing.
--
-- Both declarations here pass that test: `List Int` is `===` at the
-- element, and `List { x : Int }` is a record of primitives. So the golden
-- shows `Basics$eq` twice, and `eq$r$x` — which the checker derived for the
-- element and the emitter therefore wrote — sits there uncalled: dead
-- weight the bridge leaves behind, and one more reason it is temporary.
-- When §5.2 lands, both calls become `List$eq(<element evidence>, a, b)`
-- and `eq$r$x` becomes the evidence the second one is handed, which is
-- exactly the diff S6 should produce in this file.
--
-- The case the walk would get WRONG — an element with a `pub eq` of its own
-- — is refused rather than emitted, and lives in `tests/blackbox` because a
-- refusal has no `.mjs` to compare.
import Node exposing (Program)


pub sameInts : List Int, List Int -> Bool
sameInts a b =
    a == b


pub sameRows : List { x : Int }, List { x : Int } -> Bool
sameRows a b =
    a == b


main : Program
main =
    Node.printLines []
